From the 1988 VAX Systems and Options Guide


>       Maximum memory capacity
        320MBytes

>       CPU clock speed
        6 VAX Units of Performance (VUPS)  (this is confirmed to really 
                                            be 5.5 VUPS today)
        45ns cache cycle time     


>       I/O throughput (mb/sec)
        16MBytes/sec though two 13MByte/Sec BI busses -- the limit
        is the 8550 backplane.

>       Transactions per second
        Unavailable TPC testing wasn't conducted (to the best of my knowledge)
        at rough guess (don't hold me to this) would be 
        a 20-25 TPS rating 


>       Maximum disk capacity
        with HSCs --Terabytes
        With 4 Local KDB50s (local RA disk controllers) 8gig RA90s
                                                       16gig RA92s

 
If I had to really compair an 8550 performance wise to a modern VAX
implementation it would be the the equivilent of a VAXstation VLC
(performance wise).

The VAXstation VLC couln't use HSC technology, and would only support
up to 24MBytes of memory but direct but local disks could support up 
to 5-8 Gigabytes of storage, the CPU is rated at 6.2specmarks (slightly
faster than the 8550) and it starts at $3,000.

If I could recommend the VAXstation model 90 as real replacement,
it's rating is 34 TPS/24VUPS and according to DR labs it'll do
43 Microvax Units of Performance....

Highspeed SCSI supports about the same (5-8Gigabytes) of storage but
maximum memory is 128MBytes for this device.

Digital is selling OpenVMS licenses at the fee-simple cost of $300
each for workstations or servers these days and to get a workstation
provides the system adminstrator with that nice "COLOR" Xwindow display;-)

